--TEST--
`queryAs` takes one written type argument and is refused without it, where its `query` sibling takes none at all — `rule:core-classes/db-statement-members`, spec § 18
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The `<T>` is not decoration: it is the only thing that says what a row
// becomes, and nothing else at the call site could say it — the statement text
// is opaque and the parameters are the values going *in*. So a `queryAs` with
// no type argument has no answer to give rather than a default one, and this
// is where that is said.
use Core\Db\Derive;

#[Derive]
class Person {
    public uint $id;

    public function constructor(uint $id)
    {
        $this->id = $id;
    }
}

// The written form, on the other handle `Core\Db\Queryable` declares this
// member for: one type argument, and the answer is `Rows` at it.
class People {
    public static function everyone(Core\Db\Transaction $tx): Core\Db\Rows<Person>
    {
        return $tx->queryAs<Person>("select id from people", []);
    }
}

var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");

var $rows = $db->queryAs("select id from people", []);
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0442]: `Core\Db\Connection::queryAs` takes 1 type argument(s)
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
